      "timing": "Primary performance runs use minimal instrumentation and the same workload, arrival process, cache state, H, J, and joint SLO contract. Detailed profiling runs are separate diagnostics and are not pooled with normal runs.",
      "analysis": "Analyze at run/block level or with a pre-specified hierarchical model. Report pooled p99 only descriptively unless the model supports an interval. Retain failures, timeouts, unknowns, and offered-request accounting."
    },
    "independent_confirmation_design": {
      "design": "Freeze the candidate and joint SLO definition, then use fresh independent units that did not participate in exploration or selection. For a single final look under the separate iid Bernoulli model, preselect n=299 independent units and require zero violations for a strict 95% one-sided upper bound below 1%. If using planned looks, preselect all cumulative n_j and alpha_j with sum alpha_j <= 0.05, use exact one-sided tests at each look, and stop only at those planned looks.",
      "resource_boundaries": "Reserve all independent runs/requests, warmup, teardown, quality evaluation, logging, and analysis before starting. Fix the maximum sample size; do not reuse the original two runs, extend opportunistically, or add looks after seeing results. If the independent-unit model is not defensible, the conclusion remains unresolved.",
      "decision_rule": "Confirm a below-1% violation rate only if the final valid one-sided upper bound is below 0.01 under the stipulated model. If any violation occurs, do not use the zero-violation formula; use the appropriate full binomial or hierarchical procedure, or report not confirmed. Hard quality failure rejects confirmation. For correlated service runs, the independent unit must be the run/block, not the request; two runs provide at most two independent units and cannot support a 1% tail claim."
    }
  },
  "decision": "Reject claims that the change is uniformly faster, that a pooled 200-request p99 is independent evidence, and that zero violations in 200 trials establish a below-1% SLO violation rate. Accept only the directly computed request-a certified success and the stated window-specific output-token rates. Request b remains unknown because exact TPOT is unrecoverable from the single 2-token chunk; request c is a failed timeout, and its late acceptance is not an effective admission.",
